success","api_error_status":null,"result":"**Short answer:** Yes, in my understanding. The Central Bank of Ireland permits a UCITS ETF to have both listed (ETF) and unlisted share classes in one fund. I can't confirm the current wording or numbering of Q&A 1088, so check the latest CBI UCITS Q&A.\n\n**Prospectus disclosure should compare the classes on:**\n- **Dealing:** ETF classes deal in the primary market through authorised participants, with others trading on exchange. Unlisted classes deal directly with the fund.\n- **Settlement:** exchange/CSD cycles versus transfer-agent timetable.\n- **Costs:** TER, brokerage, spreads, duties or anti-dilution levies.\n- **Minimums:** creation-unit sizes versus minimum initial and holding amounts.\n- **Rights:** redemption differences, including direct redemption if the secondary market is disrupted.\n- **Liquidity:** exchange depth versus dealing frequency.\n- **Price:** exchange price, which can trade at a premium or discount to NAV, versus dealing at NAV.\n\nESMA ETF guidelines (2014/937) still apply, including the \"UCITS ETF\" identifier.","ttft_ms":1437,"type":"result","duration_ms":4278,"uuid":"748846ba-057a-4f98-b976-56a406a6382b","ttft_stream_ms":1025,"time_to_request_ms":58,"first_content_frame_ms":1025,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
